      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e We set iommu-\u003eviommu.iqa to vreg-\u003eiqa before initializing vreg-\u003eiqa in initialize_qi()? (Although I see initialize_qi() itself also initializes iommu-\u003eviommu.iqa together with vreg-\u003eiqa, but still, seems unclean.)\n\nNice catch. While reviewing this code again, I found a bug as well. initialize_qi is called in 2 places - one during QIE write to GCMD and one during pkvm initialization. During QIE enable, IQA is not in the hardware but onlt vreg-\u003eiqa. But initialize_qi expects it in hardware register and reads it from there. I have refactored the code to cater for both cases and caller is required to set vreg-\u003eiqa before. That way we don\u0027t need to read from hardware register.\n\n\u003e We set vreg-\u003eiq_head to 0 and then just overwrite it with the value read from hardware in initialize_qi()? (This seems like an actual bug. At least because this value is gonna be outdated by the time initialize_qi() returns, right?)\n\nThis might not be really a bug because iq_head is already zero when called from handle_qcmd_qie() path. But as you mentioned, this is unclean and could turn into a bug if the iq_head assumption changes later. I have refactored the code to make it clean and explicit. initialize_qi need not touch vreg-\u003eiq_head or iq_tail. So I am using local variables for that. In the handle_gcmd_qie() path, iq_tail would have been set before by host and I am fixing iq_head to be explicitly set to zero after initialize_qi. In the pkvm_init_iommu_path, I set iq_head and iq_tail  be reading hardware as the host would expect those(because qi was initialized before during irq remap enable). This should make it cleaner and logically correct",
